1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,070
Chai Corner now has five
outlets and a small head office.

2
00:00:04,370 --> 00:00:06,363
Meena can approve refunds now.

3
00:00:06,363 --> 00:00:09,356
But every bigger decision
still waits for Ravi.

4
00:00:09,906 --> 00:00:12,705
The college outlet wants a student offer.

5
00:00:12,705 --> 00:00:13,605
Ask Ravi.

6
00:00:13,849 --> 00:00:16,831
The bus stand needs an
extra helper tonight.

7
00:00:16,831 --> 00:00:17,731
Ask Ravi.

8
00:00:17,966 --> 00:00:22,207
By the time he answers, three days
have gone, and so have the customers.

9
00:00:22,857 --> 00:00:25,375
Ravi looks at the map of his five outlets.

10
00:00:25,725 --> 00:00:27,872
He must answer one big question.

11
00:00:27,872 --> 00:00:29,115
Who decides what?

12
00:00:29,465 --> 00:00:31,589
Keep the power here, at head office?

13
00:00:31,589 --> 00:00:33,382
Or push it out to the outlets?

14
00:00:34,032 --> 00:00:37,047
This is centralization
and decentralization.

15
00:00:37,347 --> 00:00:41,277
Centralization means the power
to decide is kept at the top.

16
00:00:41,627 --> 00:00:46,338
Decentralization means that power is
spread out, to lower levels and units.

17
00:00:46,688 --> 00:00:48,817
Last time, we saw delegation.

18
00:00:48,817 --> 00:00:51,109
One task, handed to one person.

19
00:00:51,459 --> 00:00:56,664
Decentralization is delegation across
the whole organization, done by plan.

20
00:00:57,314 --> 00:00:59,559
But this is never all or nothing.

21
00:00:59,559 --> 00:01:00,797
Picture a slider.

22
00:01:01,147 --> 00:01:04,044
All the way left, head
office decides everything.

23
00:01:04,394 --> 00:01:08,262
All the way right, every outlet
decides everything for itself.

24
00:01:08,612 --> 00:01:11,486
No real organization sits at either end.

25
00:01:11,835 --> 00:01:15,285
Henri Fayol called it a
simple question of proportion.

26
00:01:15,285 --> 00:01:17,627
Each firm must find its own point.

27
00:01:18,277 --> 00:01:20,915
So the real question is not which one.

28
00:01:20,915 --> 00:01:22,159
It is how much.

29
00:01:22,859 --> 00:01:24,650
Which way should the slider lean?

30
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,671
Lean central when the firm is small,
when rules must be the same everywhere,

31
00:01:29,671 --> 00:01:30,855
or in a crisis.

32
00:01:31,205 --> 00:01:35,916
Lean local when the firm is large,
spread over many places, or when things

33
00:01:35,916 --> 00:01:36,870
change fast.

34
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:39,259
Watch Ravi set his slider.

35
00:01:39,609 --> 00:01:43,531
The menu, the prices and the
quality rules stay at head office.

36
00:01:43,531 --> 00:01:45,827
Every cup tastes and costs the same.

37
00:01:46,177 --> 00:01:50,868
Shifts, daily stock and local offers
move out to the outlet managers.

38
00:01:51,218 --> 00:01:54,268
Meena at the bus stand
now plans her own shifts.

39
00:01:54,618 --> 00:01:58,292
And the student offer starts the
same day, not three days later.

40
00:01:58,942 --> 00:02:00,923
Where do you see this at work today?

41
00:02:01,273 --> 00:02:02,530
Central decisions.

42
00:02:02,530 --> 00:02:05,895
A bank sets its loan interest
rates at head office.

43
00:02:06,245 --> 00:02:07,433
Local decisions.

44
00:02:07,433 --> 00:02:10,997
A head nurse plans the duty
roster for her own ward.

45
00:02:11,347 --> 00:02:13,175
In a crisis, lean central.

46
00:02:13,175 --> 00:02:16,981
In a product recall, head office
takes charge of every store.

47
00:02:17,331 --> 00:02:19,739
When things change fast, lean local.

48
00:02:19,739 --> 00:02:22,713
A sales team in each
city sets its own deals.

49
00:02:23,363 --> 00:02:25,888
Each side has a price, and people feel it.

50
00:02:26,238 --> 00:02:30,117
Centralization gives tight control,
and one standard everywhere.

51
00:02:30,467 --> 00:02:35,212
But the top gets overloaded, decisions
slow down, and local managers feel

52
00:02:35,212 --> 00:02:36,414
like messengers.

53
00:02:36,764 --> 00:02:41,122
Decentralization gives fast
decisions, and trains future managers.

54
00:02:41,122 --> 00:02:42,630
People feel trusted.

55
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:46,587
But outlets can drift apart,
and mistakes are harder to spot.

56
00:02:46,937 --> 00:02:48,618
So the lesson is balance.

57
00:02:48,618 --> 00:02:51,690
Keep the core central,
and let the edges decide.

58
00:02:52,340 --> 00:02:53,274
Quick check.

59
00:02:53,274 --> 00:02:58,082
The bus stand adds a helper for a
festival rush, without asking head office.

60
00:02:58,082 --> 00:02:59,171
Which is this?

61
00:03:01,071 --> 00:03:02,159
Decentralization!

62
00:03:02,159 --> 00:03:05,881
A staffing call was made at
the outlet, close to the work.

63
00:03:06,481 --> 00:03:07,490
So, remember.

64
00:03:07,790 --> 00:03:10,563
Centralization keeps decisions at the top.

65
00:03:10,563 --> 00:03:12,804
Decentralization spreads them out.

66
00:03:13,154 --> 00:03:15,952
It is a slider, a question of proportion.

67
00:03:15,952 --> 00:03:17,856
No firm sits at either end.

68
00:03:18,206 --> 00:03:21,270
Small, uniform or in a
crisis, lean central.

69
00:03:21,270 --> 00:03:24,480
Large, spread out or fast
changing, lean local.

70
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:27,097
Next time, span of control.

71
00:03:27,097 --> 00:03:30,153
How many people can one
manager really handle?
